@page "/"

<PageTitle>Text Feed Combiner</PageTitle>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb-3">Text Feed Combiner</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Color="Color.Secondary" Class="mb-8">
    Merge several marketplace feed files into one, without the duplicate lines.
    Upload the files, pick your options, and download the result.
</MudText>

<MudPaper Elevation="0" Class="pa-8 mb-5" Style="border:1px solid var(--mud-palette-divider);">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mb-4">What it does</MudText>
    <MudList T="string" Dense="true">
        <MudListItem T="string" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.MergeType">
            Combines any number of tab-delimited or plain text feed files
        </MudListItem>
        <MudListItem T="string" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.FilterAlt">
            Drops duplicate lines, and reports how many it removed
        </MudListItem>
        <MudListItem T="string" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.Label">
            Names the output after the marketplace it recognises in the file name
        </MudListItem>
        <MudListItem T="string" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.TableRows">
            Optionally treats the first line as a header and keeps only one copy
        </MudListItem>
    </MudList>
    <MudButton Href="/combine" Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Size="Size.Large" Class="mt-5"
               EndIcon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.ArrowForward">
        Combine some files
    </MudButton>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Elevation="0" Class="pa-8" Style="border:1px solid var(--mud-palette-divider);">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mb-3">Why it exists</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Color="Color.Secondary" Class="mb-3">
        This is a web rewrite of a Windows Forms utility I wrote to merge marketplace
        feeds. In the original, every piece of logic - marketplace detection, output
        naming, duplicate removal, and file writing - lived inside a single
        drag-and-drop event handler, so none of it could be reused or tested.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Color="Color.Secondary">
        Here the same behaviour is split into small classes that know nothing about
        the browser or the file system: a rule per marketplace, a name builder, and a
        combiner that takes files in and returns a result with real statistics.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>
